From: =?UTF-8?q?Giedr=C4=97=20=C5=A0imk=C5=ABnait=C4=97?=
 <giedre.s@8devices.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:47:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: virtual-thermal-zones fix lock nesting, DT ordering
 and filter time base.
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ref.: 8devices/software/yocto/meta-8dev-qcs405#799

With CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING enabled, registering a virtual thermal zone
produces a "possible recursive locking detected" splat:

  thermal_zone_device_set_mode()     takes the virtual zone's tz->lock
    __thermal_zone_device_update()
      __thermal_zone_get_temp()
        virt_tz_read_temp()
          thermal_zone_get_temp()    takes a source zone's tz->lock

The two zones are different objects, but every zone the thermal core
registers has its tz->lock initialised at the same site, so they share
one lock class and lockdep cannot tell the nesting from recursion.  The
report is a false positive for the shipped device tree, but it is not
harmless: printing it calls debug_locks_off(), which disables lock
validation for the rest of the boot.

The nesting itself is correct and unavoidable - it happens on every
poll and every sysfs read, not just at probe - so describe it to
lockdep instead of restructuring around it.  That is only honest if the
nesting is bounded and acyclic, which the driver intended but did not
enforce: the "virtual zones cannot be used as sources" check sat in the
IS_ERR() branch of the source lookup, so it was reached only when the
referenced virtual zone was not registered yet.  Whether a virtual
source was rejected or silently accepted therefore depended on the
order of the nodes in the device tree.

Resolve the dependency graph in DT before registering anything:

 - Collect every virtual zone, resolve the virtual-to-virtual edges
   depth-first, and reject dependency cycles (including a zone that
   lists itself) and chains deeper than MAX_LOCKDEP_SUBCLASSES allows.
 - Register in order of increasing depth, so a virtual zone always
   exists before the zones that use it as a source.  Chaining becomes a
   supported configuration rather than an order-dependent accident, and
   a missing virtual source is now a reason to defer, not to reject.
 - Re-key each virtual zone's tz->lock with a private lock class and a
   subclass equal to its depth.  Edges always run from higher depth to
   lower, so the class graph is acyclic by construction and lockdep can
   validate the nesting.
 - Tear zones down in reverse depth order, so a consumer is
   unregistered before the source it reads.

Drop virt->lock: virt_tz_read_temp() is only ever reached through
__thermal_zone_get_temp(), which requires tz->lock, so the core already
serialises the readers of one zone.  With that established, the scratch
arrays can be allocated once per zone at registration, which also takes
the kcalloc()/kmemdup() out of the temperature path.

The calculation methods needed fixing independently of the locking:

 - rolling-average and exponential-average advanced their state once
   per ->get_temp() call, so their behaviour depended on how often the
   zone was read - by the core's polling, by every sysfs read, and,
   where a virtual zone is a source, by its consumers too.  Give both a
   real time base: the exponential average now derives its smoothing
   factor from the elapsed time (alpha = dt / (tau + dt)), which is
   correct at any call rate, and the rolling average is rate-limited to
   a sample interval, since a boxcar average only means anything over
   evenly spaced samples.  Both are configurable via time-constant-ms
   and sample-interval-ms.
 - weighted-average silently returned the plain average.  Implement it
   with an optional source-weights property, and carry each sample's
   weight beside it so that a source which fails to read does not shift
   the remaining weights onto the wrong zones.
 - coefficients was parsed into thermal_zone_params but never applied,
   because the core only stores slope/offset for drivers to use.  Apply
   it to the aggregate; the defaults (1, 0) leave the result unchanged.

Source lifetime is now tied to the providers.  The driver holds bare
pointers to its source zones across polls, and devres frees those zones
when a provider unbinds, so take a device link to each provider as the
zone registers.  A link that cannot be made - the provider is
unbinding, is in the middle of its own ->probe, was freed under us, or
could not be allocated - discards the whole attempt rather than leaving
a registered zone with no protection.

Report a direction to the governors.  get_tz_trend() otherwise compares
the last two temperatures, which is wrong for a rate-limited filter,
and wrong for any zone that has gone blind: update_temperature() leaves
its last two good values in place on a failed read, so the comparison
reports one fixed direction for ever and step_wise walks the cooling
state down to THERMAL_NO_TARGET on a zone that can no longer see
anything.  Report the filter's own direction, mapped through the sign
of the correction, and report stable whenever the last read found no
source at all.

A read that finds no source now returns -EAGAIN rather than -ENODATA.
update_temperature() warns once per poll, unlimited, for every error
except -EAGAIN, so a zone whose sources have all died filled the log at
the polling rate; -EAGAIN is the code the core offers for exactly this
case and what imx, sun8i, qoriq and rockchip return for a bad reading.

Match a trip's "type" case-insensitively, as thermal_of does.  It is
thermal-zones.yaml's property rather than this driver's, and rejecting
a spelling the core accepts cost the whole zone, its trips and its
cooling maps.

Reject a governor name the running kernel was not built with before it
reaches the core: __find_governor() would return NULL,
thermal_set_governor() would succeed, and the zone would finish
registering with no governor at all - which policy_show() and the
netlink handler dereference without a NULL test.

Builds clean with CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING both enabled and disabled.

Signed-off-by: Giedrė Šimkūnaitė <giedre.s@8devices.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
